WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.120
I need to come up with an attention hook

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.000
for tomorrow’s presentation. 

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:07.260
Can you help me think of some ideas?

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:11.270
OK. How about using an anecdote? 

00:00:012.000 --> 00:00:14.060
People love stories. 

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.260
Tell them a short story to pique their interest. 

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.010
You can say “Before we begin today,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.030
let me share a story with you.”

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:27.280
But I am not good at telling stories.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.120 
Can I use a quote from a famous person or book?

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.250
Of course. For example, you can say,

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.180 
“There is a famous quote by Henry Ford that says,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.200 
Whether you think that you can,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.180
or that you can’t, 

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.080
you are usually right.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000 
That is to encourage you to be confident 

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.150
and think positively about yourself.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.250
That’s a nice quote. 

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.050
Can I use a question to get their attention? 

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.020
Something like,“Let me start by asking you a question.” 

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.270
But I don’t want to embarrass anyone

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.230
by asking the wrong question. 

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.000
Yes, so remember not to ask anything too personal.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.040 
People might be offended by being asked

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.040
a personal question in public.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.080
Is it helpful to make connections with your audience?

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.210 
For example

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.250
“It’s nice to see so many familiar faces here.” 

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.270
Yes, the audience will feel connected to you

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.000
and interact positively with you.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:35.130
You can also tell a joke to break the ice.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.290
For example, “everyone is gifted

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.210
but some people just never open their packages”. 

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.030
Ah, that would definitely grab the audience’s attention 

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.060
because you have to think twice to get that joke. 

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:55.040
But be careful to choose an appropriate joke.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:59.050 
Use humor properly, or it might backfire.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.060 
Don’t overuse it. 

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.070
Take this joke for example: 

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.200
Do you know how much a polar bear weighs?

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.050
No. How much does a polar bear weigh?

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.220
Just enough to break the ice!

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.020
Ha, ha, ha. That’s funny. 

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.120
The same as the polar bear

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.160
your attention hook shouldn’t be too heavy, 

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.080
but just enough to break the ice.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:28.220
Thanks. That’s a good point. 

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.170
Wait! I just thought of a perfect

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.270
icebreaker for my presentation. 

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.000
What is it?

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.100
Well, you’ll have to come to my

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.000
presentation tomorrow to find out.



